---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="DMARC Alignment: Relaxed vs Strict Explained"
  description="DMARC alignment explained: how the From domain must match SPF or DKIM authenticated identities, relaxed vs strict modes, and why mail can pass SPF but fail DMARC."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">DMARC Alignment</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DMARC Alignment: Relaxed vs Strict Explained</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      DMARC alignment is the rule that connects authentication results to the domain the recipient actually sees. A message can pass SPF and pass DKIM and still fail DMARC — because the authenticated domains do not match the visible From domain. This page explains why that happens and what to change.
    </p>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2>The three domains involved</h2>
      <p>Every email carries three domain identities that DMARC compares:</p>
      <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Identity</th><th>Where it appears</th><th>What it controls</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">From domain</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The visible <code class="font-mono">From:</code> header</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">What the recipient sees; the domain DMARC protects</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">SPF domain</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The <code class="font-mono">Return-Path</code> (envelope from)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Which server is authorized to send</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">DKIM domain</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The <code class="font-mono">d=</code> tag in the DKIM-Signature</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Who cryptographically signed the message</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>How DMARC uses these domains</h2>
      <p>
        DMARC checks whether the SPF-authenticated domain or the DKIM-authenticated domain <strong class="text-ink">aligns</strong> with the From domain. Only one needs to align — DMARC passes if <em>either</em> aligned SPF pass <em>or</em> aligned DKIM pass exists. It does not require both. This is defined in RFC 7489 <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7489#section-3.1" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">§3.1</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Relaxed vs strict alignment</h2>
      <p>
        The alignment mode is controlled by the <code class="font-mono">aspf</code> (for SPF) and <code class="font-mono">adkim</code> (for DKIM) tags in your DMARC record. Both default to <strong class="text-ink">relaxed</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>SPF alignment rule</th><th>DKIM alignment rule</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Relaxed (default)</td>
              <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The SPF domain and From domain must share the same organizational domain (e.g., <code class="font-mono">mail.example.com</code> aligns with <code class="font-mono">example.com</code>)</td>
              <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The DKIM <code class="font-mono">d=</code> domain and From domain must share the same organizational domain</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">Strict</td>
              <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The SPF domain must exactly match the From domain</td>
              <td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">The DKIM <code class="font-mono">d=</code> domain must exactly match the From domain</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Synthetic examples (labeled, not customer evidence)</h2>

      <h3>Example 1: SPF passes but DMARC fails (alignment failure)</h3>
      <pre class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">From: marketing@brand.com
Return-Path: bounce@esp123.net  ← SPF evaluated against esp123.net
DKIM-Signature: d=esp123.net    ← DKIM signed by esp123.net

SPF result:  pass  (sender IP is authorized by esp123.net's SPF)
DKIM result: pass  (signature verifies against esp123.net's key)
DMARC result: fail  (neither esp123.net aligns with brand.com)</pre>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink-faint">Synthetic example. This is the most common DMARC failure: the ESP sends mail using its own domain for Return-Path and DKIM, not the brand's domain.</p>

      <h3>Example 2: SPF fails but DMARC passes via aligned DKIM</h3>
      <pre class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">From: newsletter@brand.com
Return-Path: bounce@other.net    ← SPF fails (IP not authorized)
DKIM-Signature: d=brand.com     ← DKIM signed by brand.com

SPF result:  fail  (IP not authorized by other.net's SPF)
DKIM result: pass  (signature verifies; d=brand.com aligns with From: brand.com)
DMARC result: pass  (aligned DKIM pass exists)</pre>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink-faint">Synthetic example. DMARC passes because one aligned authentication (DKIM) succeeded — SPF failure alone does not cause DMARC failure when DKIM is aligned.</p>

      <h3>Example 3: Forwarded mail failing SPF but passing via DKIM</h3>
      <pre class="mt-2 rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">From: user@brand.com
Return-Path: nobody@forwarder.com ← SPF fails (forwarder not authorized)
DKIM-Signature: d=brand.com      ← Original signature survives forwarding

SPF result:  fail  (forwarder's IP is not in brand.com's SPF)
DKIM result: pass  (original DKIM signature intact, d=brand.com aligned)
DMARC result: pass  (aligned DKIM pass)</pre>
      <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink-faint">Synthetic example. This is why DKIM alignment is important even when SPF is set up correctly: forwarding breaks SPF but preserves DKIM signatures.</p>

      <h2>Fixing alignment failures</h2>
      <p>
        The fix depends on your sending setup. There is no single universal DNS change — in many cases, the configuration must be made in the email provider's platform, not just in DNS.
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Identify which sender is failing.</strong> Check your DMARC aggregate reports (or send test messages and read the <code class="font-mono">Authentication-Results</code> header) to see which service's mail is failing alignment.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Configure the ESP to use your domain.</strong> Most ESPs (SendGrid, Mailchimp, HubSpot, etc.) support custom Return-Path (bounce domain) and custom DKIM signing. Enable both, using your domain, so alignment passes. See your provider's specific setup guide — links are in our <a href="/email-authentication/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">ESP setup directory</a>.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Verify with a test message.</strong> Send a test from the ESP and inspect the <code class="font-mono">Authentication-Results</code> header. Both <code class="font-mono">spf=pass</code> and <code class="font-mono">dkim=pass</code> should appear with your domain.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Check alignment mode.</strong> If you use subdomains for sending, relaxed alignment (the default) is usually sufficient. Strict alignment is rarely needed unless your DMARC policy explicitly requires exact matching.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>What our DNS-based tools can and cannot determine</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/dmarc-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC Record Checker</a> inspects your published DMARC record (policy, reporting, alignment mode tags). It cannot evaluate a specific message's alignment — that requires reading the <code class="font-mono">Authentication-Results</code> header on a received message. DNS-based tools verify that the policy is correct; message-level tools verify that the policy is being satisfied.
      </p>

      <h2>Related</h2>
      <ul class="grid gap-2">
        <li><a href="/dmarc/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC: the complete guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/spf-record/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">SPF records explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dkim/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DKIM explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/dmarc-record-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DMARC Record Checker (free tool)</a></li>
        <li><a href="/email-header-analyzer/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Email Header Analyzer (free tool)</a></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mt-6 text-xs text-ink-faint">
        Primary reference: <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7489" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft" rel="noopener">RFC 7489 — Domain-based Message Authentication, Reporting &amp; Conformance</a>. Synthetic examples are labeled and illustrate the alignment rules; they are not output from any tool or customer data.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
